---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Brief = `Our sensor logger keeps recording nonsense and nobody notices
until the report is wrong. I want it to refuse a value that
will not fit, and say so, instead of quietly storing garbage.`;

const SampleOutput = `  200 stored in 8 bits
  255 stored in 8 bits
  256 does not fit in 8 bits (max 255)
  1000 does not fit in 8 bits (max 255)
  -3 does not fit in 8 bits (max 255)
with 16 bits:
  200 stored in 16 bits
  255 stored in 16 bits
  256 stored in 16 bits
  1000 stored in 16 bits
  -3 does not fit in 16 bits (max 65535)`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-set-7" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back to Set 7
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge: Overflow Guard</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Brief</h2>

          <Code code={Brief} frame="code" lang="txt" title="What they asked for" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Write your requirements list before you write any code.</b> Input,
            calculate, output, and anything you had to decide for yourself. See{" "}
            <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-set-7">Set 7</a> for what that looks like.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="notes">
          <h2>Notes</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>The largest value <i>n</i> bits can hold is one less than 2 to the <i>n</i>. Calculate that; do not hardcode 255.</li>
            <li>The bit width is a <b>parameter</b>. The same procedure has to work for 8 bits and 16.</li>
            <li>Reject anything below zero as well. Say in your requirements list what you decided about negatives and why.</li>
            <li><b>Return something the caller can test.</b> Printing a complaint is not enough if the caller then stores the value anyway.</li>
            <li>This is 2.16's overflow, caught instead of suffered.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>Sample Output</h2>

          <Code code={SampleOutput} frame="terminal" title="Sample Output" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Widening to 16 bits rescues three of the five and not the fourth.</b> A
            negative reading is a different kind of wrong, and no number of bits fixes
            it &mdash; that distinction belongs in your requirements list.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your requirements list and your program file, named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">cc-overflow-guard</b>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
